  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 301 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nWe'll start to feel the impacts of the incoming trough on Monday\nas southeasterly winds become gusty near 30-35 mph by late\nmorning with the strongest gusts along and west of Highway 65.\nRain chances begin to increase overnight Monday into Tuesday \n(50-90%).\n\nThere is still a Marginal to Slight risk of severe weather \npossible for areas along and west of I-49 with the Slight being\nfurther west. Timing for severe weather is Monday afternoon/ \nevening. Right now, the best severe risk still appears to be \nover central KS, with the western CWA getting clipped. This is \nlikely due to the base of the trough going through that area \nbefore the trough becomes elongated as it moves over us due to \nanother incoming mid-level trough over the Great Lakes region. \nAll modes of severe weather are possible with this system as \nsupercells will initiate first just to our west. By the time it \ngets to our area, guidance is showing the convection forming a \nline of storms as it pushes through. This storm mode would favor\nmore of a QLCS than supercells. Though, the instability is \nstill a bit lacking, the wind shear and SRH will be conducive \nfor severe weather. If storms stay severe as they enter our CWA,\nthey will likely occur west of I-49 with storms becoming less \nsevere as they push further east into a less conducive \nenvironment. KS and OK may see the majority of the severe \nweather, with us seeing some rumbles of thunder and beneficial \nrainfall of 1 to 2 inches through Tuesday. Rain will taper off \nby Tuesday morning. \n\nThe low pressure system that moves through at the beginning of\nthe week will drop our temperatures, but only by about 10\ndegrees. Though, it will bring them closer to average for this\ntime of year with highs in the 60s and lows in the 40s for the\nrest of the week. Another low pressure system will bring low-end\nrain chances by mid-week (15-20%).",
